## 3. Security Controls

> **Security Controls:** Specific measures that fulfill **Control Objectives** (statements of a desired security state determined by business leaders to protect CIA).  
> **Gap Analysis:** An evaluation of current controls to find areas that do not address objectives.

### Control Categories

| Category | Focus | Examples |
|---|---|---|
| **Technical** | Enforces CIA in the digital space | Firewalls, intrusion prevention systems (IPS), encryption |
| **Operational** | Day-to-day processes in place | User access reviews, log monitoring |
| **Managerial** | Procedural mechanisms focused on the risk management process | Risk assessments, security governance, change management |
| **Physical** | Physical world security measures | Fences, locks, lighting, alarms |

### Control Types

| Control Type | Purpose | Examples |
|---|---|---|
| **Preventive** | Stops a security incident from occurring | Firewalls, encryption |
| **Deterrent** | Discourages an attacker from attempting an attack | Guard dogs, barbed wire, warning signs |
| **Detective** | Identifies and alerts to an ongoing or past incident | Intrusion Detection Systems (IDS) |
| **Corrective** | Restores systems after an incident | Restoring backups, applying patches |
| **Compensating** | Mitigates risk when primary security policies cannot be met | Alternative controls or temporary exceptions |
| **Directive** | Mandates security actions by policy | Policies, procedures, standard operating guidelines |

